## Solution

**Answer leak check:** The problem statement contains no leaked answer, solution sketch, or formalization notes. Proceeding.

---

Let $C$ be the unit circle $|z|=1$. The hypothesis is
$$|f(z)-z^{2}|\le |z^{2}|=1 \qquad (|z|=1).$$

Define $g(z)=f(z)-z^{2}$. Then $g$ is analytic on and inside $C$, and $|g(z)|\le 1$ on $|z|=1$. By the **maximum modulus principle**, $|g(z)|\le 1$ throughout the closed unit disk $\overline{\mathbb D}$.

Since $f(z)=z^{2}+g(z)$, we have
$$f'(z)=2z+g'(z),\qquad f'\!\left(\tfrac14\right)=\tfrac12+g'\!\left(\tfrac14\right).$$

So the problem reduces to: **maximize $\bigl|\tfrac12+g'\!\left(\tfrac14\right)\bigr|$** over all analytic $g:\mathbb D\to\overline{\mathbb D}$.

---

### Bounding $|g'(a)|$

By the **Schwarz–Pick lemma**, for $g$ analytic with $|g|\le 1$ in $\mathbb D$ and any $a\in\mathbb D$,
$$|g'(a)|\le \frac{1-|g(a)|^{2}}{1-|a|^{2}}\le \frac{1}{1-|a|^{2}}.$$

At $a=\tfrac14$:
$$\left|g'\!\left(\tfrac14\right)\right|\le \frac{1}{1-\frac{1}{16}}=\frac{1}{\frac{15}{16}}=\frac{16}{15}.$$

Hence
$$\left|f'\!\left(\tfrac14\right)\right|=\left|\tfrac12+g'\!\left(\tfrac14\right)\right|\le \tfrac12+\left|g'\!\left(\tfrac14\right)\right|\le \tfrac12+\frac{16}{15}=\frac{15+32}{30}=\frac{47}{30}.$$

---

### Sharpness — the bound is attained

Consider the Möbius (Blaschke) automorphism
$$g(z)=\frac{z-\frac14}{1-\frac14 z}.$$

It maps $\mathbb D$ onto $\mathbb D$, so $|g(z)|\le 1$ in $\mathbb D$ and $|g(z)|=1$ on $|z|=1$. Thus $f(z)=z^{2}+g(z)$ satisfies $|f(z)-z^{2}|=|g(z)|=1\le|z^{2}|$ on $C$.

Compute $g'\!\left(\tfrac14\right)$:
$$g'(z)=\frac{\bigl(1-\tfrac14 z\bigr)-\bigl(z-\tfrac14\bigr)\bigl(-\tfrac14\bigr)}{\bigl(1-\tfrac14 z\bigr)^{2}}=\frac{1-\tfrac14 z+\tfrac14 z-\tfrac{1}{16}}{\bigl(1-\tfrac14 z\bigr)^{2}}=\frac{\frac{15}{16}}{\bigl(1-\tfrac14 z\bigr)^{2}}.$$

At $z=\tfrac14$:
$$g'\!\left(\tfrac14\right)=\frac{\frac{15}{16}}{\bigl(\frac{15}{16}\bigr)^{2}}=\frac{16}{15}\in\mathbb R^{+}.$$

Therefore
$$f'\!\left(\tfrac14\right)=\tfrac12+\frac{16}{15}=\frac{47}{30},\qquad \left|f'\!\left(\tfrac14\right)\right|=\frac{47}{30}.$$

The upper bound $\tfrac{47}{30}$ is achieved, so it is the maximum.

---

$$\boxed{\dfrac{47}{30}}$$

